What upstream lacks (hermes-agent, apps/desktop): the main process's REST
reads are never conditional. The renderer re-reads the open chat's history
tail (GET /api/sessions/<id>/messages?limit=120&order=latest) on every
sessions.changed tick, which any write to the profile's state.db fires
(other chats, cron, subagents): for a long chat ~380 KB every ~10 s while
nothing in it changed (Dave's laptop, 2026-11-27: 124 MB of 189 MB in
5.5 h).

This patch (Nix): fetchJson and the OAuth-session reads keep the body of
each tagged 2xx GET (ETag) per URL and send If-None-Match with its tag the
next time; a 304 is answered from the held body, so callers see the same
200 they would have read. Whether anything changed is the server's call,
from the body it would send now (core's hermes-dashboard-messages-etag
patch hashes the page), so no write can be missed: appends, tool rows,
edits, deletions and compaction all change the tag. Fallbacks: an error
status, an HTML or unparsable body, or a tag-less response drops the held
body (the next read is a full one); a 304 to our tag whose body is no
longer held is read again once without a validator; a 304 to a request
without our tag stays the 3xx it is (rejected as before), so a server
that always answers 304 never loops. Only GETs; a caller's own
If-None-Match wins. Bounded: 8M chars per body, 32M in all, least
recently used first; cleared on sign-out and when a connection is
removed. The OAuth reads skip Chromium's HTTP cache (no second, on-disk
copy). A 304/204 is never gunzipped.

Tests: electron/json-revalidation.test.ts.

